The map fromCC sends an element s of the sum neighbourhood system of finite strings to the element of the string system that contains a neighbourhood when that neighbourhood is the set of all strings, or it is the singleton of the empty string and s contains the unit neighbourhood, or it is the letter-prefixed copy of a neighbourhood of the string system and s contains the corresponding tagged copy. Each such neighbourhood belongs to the string system: the whole set is the cone of the empty string, the singleton of the empty string is a neighbourhood, and a prefixed copy of a neighbourhood is a neighbourhood. The master neighbourhood belongs to the element because it is the whole token set. For intersections, both neighbourhoods may be the whole set, the empty-string singleton, or a prefixed copy. Two copies of the whole set, or the whole set met with either of the other forms, return the smaller neighbourhood. Two copies of the empty-string singleton return that singleton. The empty-string singleton and a tagged copy cannot both belong to s, because their intersection in the sum system is empty, while every neighbourhood of s is nonempty. Two tagged copies with the same letter intersect in the tagged copy of the intersection, which is a neighbourhood, and the prefixed copies intersect in the prefixed copy of that intersection. Two tagged copies with different letters intersect emptily, which is again impossible. For upward closure, a larger neighbourhood is the whole set, the empty-string singleton, or a prefixed copy. Inclusion of the whole set forces the larger neighbourhood to be the whole set. The empty-string singleton stays the empty-string singleton, and it cannot contain a prefixed copy because the empty string lies in no prefixed copy. A larger prefixed copy must carry the same first letter, because a string from the smaller nonempty copy lies in it, and the tagged membership is then enlarged because the prefixed inclusion is a tagged inclusion.
